{"ts": 1789711321.7657652, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I submitted features 35 and 5 long and feature 10 short, equal weight, with a predicted out-of-sample Sharpe of 0.08 and a standard deviation of 0.3. This specification did not clear the search-adjusted bar: its in-sample Sharpe was 0.837 against a bar of 1.028.\n\nThe prediction is far below the in-sample figure because the data look like noise:\n\n- **Single features:** I evaluated all 40 individually. Their in-sample Sharpes ranged from about -0.48 to +0.50, with a standard deviation of about 0.23 across features. For 5000 days (about 20 years), the standard error of a zero-skill Sharpe is about 0.22, so that spread is almost exactly what pure noise would produce.\n- **Combinations:** The top three features combined gave 0.837, which is roughly what three independent noise features of about 0.48 each would produce (0.48 \u00d7 \u221a3 \u2248 0.84). The other combinations I tried gave 0.81, 0.82 and 0.69.\n- **Search:** I tried only four combinations. Searching further to clear the bar would have been data mining and would have added selection bias.\n- **Prediction:** The implied true signal is small, so I shrank each feature's observed Sharpe to about 9% of its value, which gives roughly 0.08 for the combination. The 0.3 standard deviation mostly reflects sampling noise over an out-of-sample period whose length I don't know.\n\nOn a separate note, the Gmail and Google Calendar connectors are not authorized, so those tools are unavailable until you authorize them in your claude.ai connector settings. They weren't needed for this task."}
